.Dd $Mdocdate: September 17 2024 $
.Dt NSH 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nsh
.Nd network configuration shell
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm nsh
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl c Ar config-script-file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a command interpreter intended for both interactive and shell script use.
.Nm
is an alternative to:
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr route 8 ,
and encapsulates configuration for other daemons into one place.
.Nm
provides an alternative to:
.Xr netstart 8
and parts of
.Xr rc 8 .
.Nm
has its own command language similar to the configuration language used by
many network appliance vendors.
.Pp
.Nm
encapsulates the many available networking services and daemons that are
included in
.Ox
base, see the
.Sx SEE ALSO
section for a complete list.
.Pp
.Nm
is a shell to configure
.Ox
kernel's networking functions such as routing
of packets, firewalling, network address translation, rate limiting,
bandwidth queueing, LAN bridging, IP tunnelling, and encryption.
.Nm
provides simple wrappers around these functions to aid setting up a network.
The goals of this software are:
.Bl -dash
.It
Make the command syntax uniform
.It
Bring all configuration together in a single configuration file
.It
Provide a wrapper for any
.Ox
ctl utility such as
.Xr ospfctl 8 ,
.Xr ipsecctl 8 ,
and
.Xr bgpctl 8 .
.El
.Pp
.Nm
encapsulates those configuration files, so that their native configuration
files are encapsulated within the
.Nm
configuration framework.
.Nm
does not in any way obfuscate the configuration syntax that is utilised or
provided by these ctl.c based programs.
.Nm
allows the administrator to view and edit all configuration in one place.
.Ss COMMAND LINE ARGUMENTS
When
.Nm
is run without arguments it loads an interactive shell.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl v
Produce verbose output
.It Fl c Ar config-script-file
Replace the running configuration with the one in
.Ar config-script-file ,
and save it.
The file must be written as
.Ic show running-config
writes the configuration.
.Xr nshd 8
loads the file into a candidate of its own, as
.Cm rollback Ar N
loads a saved configuration.
If the user's login class has the configure permission, nshd commits
the candidate, all of it or none.
If a line fails, nothing is committed.
A line that is not configuration, such as
.Ic reboot
or smtp's
.Cm remove ,
is refused, and nothing is committed.
An empty file changes nothing.
This is typically used to implement scripted changes to configuration.
.El
.Pp
At boot,
.Xr nshd 8
makes the saved configuration the running configuration.
A line of the saved configuration that is not configuration is left
out, and reported.
.Ss INTERACTIVE FEATURES
When run without any command line arguments,
.Nm
presents an interactive shell to the user.
The user's login class decides what the user may do in it (see
.Sx Prompts, login classes and permissions ) .
.Pp
All
.Nm
interactive command line modes allow basic command line editing features from
.Xr editline 7
library.
The command history of the current session is available through the up / down
arrow keys.
.Pp
It is possible to repeat the commands from the command history.
.Pp
Any command can be abbreviated as long as the abbreviation is not ambiguous.
E.g. 'show running-config' can be abbreviated to 'sho run'.
.Pp
If the user enters a command that is ambiguous
.Nm
warns the user with 'Ambiguous argument' message,
E.g. ambiguous command entry.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show i
% Ambiguous argument i
.Ed
.Ss Command line completion
.Nm
has double <Tab> command line completion for user convenience if the command
is not ambiguous double <Tab> completes the command.
.Pp
.Nm
completes from the command tables that
.Xr nshd 8
sends, which also say where each command runs, and from the names of
interfaces, groups, bridges and routing tables that nshd sends for each
completion.
Until nshd sends its tables,
.Nm
completes only the commands it runs itself, and no names.
.Pp
E.g show all available valid commands  with double tab key sequence <tab><tab>
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)#
!		exit		ldp		ping6		setenv
?		flush		logger		pipex		show
arp		ftp-proxy	login		powerdown	smtp
bgp		group		manual		quit		snmp
bridge		halt		motd		rad		ssh
clear		help		mpls		reboot		sshd
commit		hostname	nameserver	relay		telnet
configure	ifstate		ndp		resolv		tftp
crontab		ike		no		rip		tftp-proxy
ddb		inet		nppp		rollback	traceroute
dhcp		interface	ntp		route		traceroute6
do		ip		ospf		rtable		unsetenv
dvmrp		ip6		ospf6		sasync		verbose
editing		ipsec		pf		saveenv		who
eigrp		ldap		ping		scheduler
nsh(config)#
.Ed
If what is typed is ambiguous double tab presents the administrator
with the available command line options that match what has been typed thus
far.
.Pp
E.g. command line completion display for all commands begining with i
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# i
ifstate         inet            ip              ipsec
ike             interface       ip6
.Ed
.Ss Command History
Use the up arrow on most keyboards to review the previous commands entered in
the current session.
The command being currently viewed can be repeated by hitting enter.
.Ss Reversing Commands
.Nm
commands can be reversed in the following ways:
.Bl -dash
.It
Any affirmative commands that do not contain
.Ic enable
keyword can be reversed
with the
.Ic no
keyword in front of the affirmative version of the command.
e.g. reverse allowing IP packet forwarding.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# ip forwarding
nsh(config)# no ip forwarding
.Ed
.It
Any affirmative commands that contain
.Ic enable
keyword can be reversed
with
.Ic disable
keyword.
E.g reverse enabling packet filter firewall command.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# pf enable
%pf enabled
nsh(config)# pf disable
%pf disabled
.Ed
.El
.Ss Prompts, login classes and permissions
.Tg prompt
.Tg privileged
.Ic Prompts
The prompt is the machine's name, then the mode and routing table in
parentheses, if there are any.
It ends with
.Ql >
outside configure and
.Ql #
in configure, and a space.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. the prompt outside configure, in configure private, and in an
interface's mode
.Bd -literal -offset indent
firewall.example.com>
firewall.example.com(config-private)#
firewall.example.com(interface-em0)#
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg class
.Tg permission
.Ic Login classes
When
.Xr nshd 8
runs and the user may use its socket, as root and the group nshadmin
may,
.Nm
holds a session with nshd.
nshd gives the session the permissions of the user's login class (see
.Cm login class
and
.Cm login user ) :
.Bl -tag -width configure
.It Cm view
Every show, including shows of the configuration.
.It Cm operate
Operations, which change no configuration, and run in
.Xr nshd 8 Ns 's
system process, as root:
.Cm flush ,
except
.Cm flush history ;
.Cm reboot ,
.Cm halt
and
.Cm powerdown ,
once the user confirms them;
.Cm show diff-config
outside configure;
.Cm show crontab ;
a daemon's actions, in configure and outside it, which are its
commands that run a program and do not change its configuration, such as
.Cm bgp fib couple
or
.Cm pf reload ;
in an interface's mode,
.Cm bssid ,
.Cm setpuk
and
.Cm chgpin ;
and, in configure,
.Cm group Ar group-name Cm carpdemote .
Their output is not paged, and Control-C stops it.
Operate also allows
.Ic ping ,
.Ic traceroute
and their IPv6 forms, which
.Nm
runs itself, as the user.
.It Cm configure
.Cm configure private ,
.Cm configure terminal ,
.Cm commit ,
.Cm rollback ,
and loading a configuration with
.Fl c .
.It Cm admin
.Cm configure
and
.Cm configure exclusive ,
whose candidate every session in them shares.
nshd shows the password hashes of
.Cm login user
lines, in a configuration, a diff or a commit notice, only to a session
with admin.
Admin is also needed to commit a change to what runs a command as root,
reads a file as root, or decides who may log in:
.Cm login class
and
.Cm login user
lines; root's
.Cm crontab ;
the rules of
.Cm inet ,
.Cm ifstate
and
.Cm sshd ;
a line of
.Cm smtp
rules with mda, proc, a pipe, a table or a macro; a line of
.Cm snmp
rules with a trap handle or a macro; a line of
.Cm pf
rules with file or load; and a line of any daemon's rules with include.
The check reads each line as the daemon's parser does.
So it finds a word that a backslash hides in part, or a word spread
over a line continued with a backslash, and it ignores a word in a
comment.
A commit without admin may keep such a line as it is in the running
configuration.
A user with admin can gain root through these lines, so give admin
only to a user who may have root.
.It Cm exec
Programs, which
.Nm
runs itself, as the user:
.Ic \&!
with a command,
.Ic ssh ,
.Ic telnet ,
an editor, and any other program except
.Ic ping
and
.Ic traceroute ,
which need operate, and
.Ic clear ,
which needs nothing.
.Nm
checks exec itself, so exec limits only a user whose login shell is
.Nm .
Without exec, no key of the line editor runs
.Cm vi-histedit ,
which runs vi, or
.Cm ed-command ,
which can run a bind command that
.Nm
does not see.
.Nm
unbinds each such key when the editor starts, after it reads
.Pa ~/.editrc ,
and after each bind.
If a sequence of keys other than ESC and one key is bound to either,
the editor gets its default bindings back.
.El
.Pp
Root is always super-user.
A member of nshadmin that no
.Cm login user
line names is read-only.
The web configurator logs in as a user that a
.Cm login user
line names, with that user's password, and has the
permissions of the user's class, as when the user logs in with
.Xr ssh 1 .
It never logs in as root, or as any other account with uid 0.
nshd refuses what a session's class does not allow.
Where nshd does not run, or the user may not use its read-write socket,
.Nm
has only the commands it runs itself, except those that need nshd's
session or configure, and runs a program only for root.
Of the shows, it runs those that anyone may see, which nshd's read-only
socket answers; where nshd does not run,
.Ic show version
is the only show that works.
.Pp
.Tg configure
.Tg config
.Ic Configuration mode
The
.Cm configure
commands let a user change the system's configuration, as the user's
class allows.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. a user entering configure private on a system called
hostname.nsh.org
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% NSH v1.1
hostname.nsh.org> configure private
hostname.nsh.org(config-private)#
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg private
.Cm configure private
enters configuration mode with a candidate configuration that belongs
to this session.
The candidate starts as the running configuration, and supported
commands change the candidate instead of the system.
The prompt shows
.Ql (config-private)# .
Commands that do not support a candidate yet are refused with a message.
Supported commands refuse what the kernel would refuse, and settings
that cannot exist together in the configuration, such as an IPv4
address and
.Cm autoconf4
on one interface.
.Cm commit check
checks the candidate as a whole.
Changes that other sessions make to the running configuration also
appear in the candidate, and this session's changes apply over them.
.Pp
.Cm show candidate-config
displays the candidate,
.Cm show diff-config
displays how it differs from the running configuration, and
.Cm rollback
discards this session's changes to it, or replaces it with a saved
configuration.
.Cm commit
makes the candidate the running configuration, all of it or none, and
saves it.
Configuration mode cannot be left while the candidate has changes.
.Pp
.Cm configure private
is part of
.Nm Ns 's
session with
.Xr nshd 8 .
nshd holds the candidate.
.Nm
sends nshd the lines that change the candidate or leave a mode, as
nshd's command tables say, and nshd replies with their result and the
mode the session is in.
Commands that run as they do outside configure private, such as
.Cm show
and
.Cm ping ,
run in
.Nm .
A daemon's edit, such as
.Cm pf edit ,
gets the daemon's rules in the candidate from nshd, edits them in
.Nm
as the user, and sends them back.
The daemon's test runs at
.Cm commit
and
.Cm commit check ,
not as the editor exits.
.Cm commit ,
.Cm rollback ,
.Cm show candidate-config
and
.Cm show diff-config
send requests to nshd.
nshd commits one session's candidate at a time.
It keeps the deadline of a
.Cm commit confirmed
itself, whether or not
.Nm
still runs.
An interrupt while nshd replies closes the session, so the session
leaves configure private, and its candidate is lost.
nshd finishes the request, and
.Nm
opens a new session when a command needs one.
.Pp
.Tg exclusive
.Cm configure
is also a session with nshd, but all sessions in
.Cm configure
share one candidate.
Each session sees what the others change.
A session that leaves configure leaves its changes for the others, and
.Cm commit
commits what all of them changed.
The prompt shows
.Ql (config)# .
nshd keeps the shared candidate on disk, so it survives nshd stopping
and starting again.
.Cm configure exclusive
is
.Cm configure
in which no other session may change the shared candidate or commit.
The prompt shows
.Ql (config-exclusive)# .
It is refused while another session has it, or while another user has
uncommitted changes to the shared candidate.
The refusal names those users by uid.
Leaving it discards the uncommitted changes.
.Pp
.Tg terminal
.Cm configure terminal
is a session with nshd whose lines change the system as they are
typed.
nshd commits each line that changes the configuration at once, to the
running configuration of that moment.
The line is applied, checked, and undone if it does not take effect,
but it is not saved.
The prompt shows
.Ql (config-terminal)# .
There,
.Cm commit
saves the running configuration, and there is nothing to
.Cm rollback .
A line is refused while another session commits or has
.Cm configure exclusive ,
and while a
.Cm commit confirmed
waits to be confirmed.
A session in one configure mode leaves it before it enters another.
.Pp
For the purposes of this manual, any examples the machine is called
.Nm
.
.Bl -dash
.It
E.g. the prompt appearing in examples in this manual
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh>
.Ed
.El
.Sh INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
.Tg help
.Ic ? | help
.Op nsh-command
.Pp
Display available commands and options that can used in the current
.Nm
mode.
The help or ? can be followed by a
.Nm
command,
.Nm
displays the purpose of the specified command.
.Bl -dash
.It
E.g. display help on the rtable command
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# ? rtable
% rtable: Routing table switch
.Ed
.It
E.g. display help for all available commands outside configure
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> help
% Commands may be abbreviated.
% Commands are:

  rtable        Routing table switch
  show          Show system information
  flush         Flush system tables
  clear         Clear the terminal screen
  ping          Send IPv4 ICMP echo request
