nsh(config)#
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg manual
.Ic manual
.Op Ar search tag
.Pp
Display the
.Nm
manual page.
If a
.Ar search tag
is specified then jump to the first section matching this tag if one
or more matching tags exist.
.Pp
.Tg hostname
.Ic hostname
.Op Ar hostname
.Pp
hostname without arguments displays the current configured hostname
of the system.
hostname with an argument will set hostname to the argument's value.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. set hostname to the myfirewallname.com .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# hostname myfirewallname.com
nsh(config)# commit
% Saving configuration
myfirewallname.com(config)#
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg login
.Op no
.Ic login class Ar name
.Op Ar permission ...
.Pp
Define a login class, a set of permissions:
.Cm view ,
.Cm operate ,
.Cm configure ,
.Cm admin
and
.Cm exec
(see
.Sx Prompts, login classes and permissions ) .
Three classes are built in and cannot be changed:
.Cm read-only
has view,
.Cm operator
has view and operate, and
.Cm super-user
has all five.
.Pp
.Op no
.Ic login user Ar name Cm class Ar class
.Op Cm password Ar hash | Cm plain-text-password
.Pp
Give the account
.Ar name
a login class, one that is built in or one a
.Cm login class
line defines.
If the account does not exist,
.Xr nshd 8
makes it when the line is committed, with the password
.Ar hash ,
.Nm
as its shell, and the group nshadmin, so that it can use nshd.
The hash is a bcrypt hash, as
.Xr encrypt 1
makes.
In place of the hash, the word
.Cm plain-text-password
has
.Nm
ask for the password twice, without showing it, and send the
password's bcrypt hash in place of the word, as Junos does.
Only a line typed in
.Nm
at a terminal may say
.Cm plain-text-password .
Removing the line deletes an account that nshd made, but keeps its
home directory.
.Pp
An account that nshd did not make, such as one an administrator made by
hand, gets its class and nothing else.
Its line accepts no password, and removing the line leaves the account
unchanged.
No line may name root or any other account with uid 0, which is always
super-user, or an account whose name begins with an underscore, since
those belong to daemons.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. Let alice, who has no account, operate and configure the system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# login class netops view operate configure
nsh(config)# login user alice class netops password $2b$10$kKSJFDd....
.Ed
.It
e.g. Let bob, who has no account, operate the system, with a password
that
.Nm
asks for:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# login user bob class operator password plain-text-password
New password:
Retype new password:
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg clear
.Ic clear
.Pp
Wipe contents of terminal and scrollback buffer and present a
.Nm
prompt at the first line of the active terminal.
See also
.Xr clear 1
for more information.
.Pp
.Tg rtable
.Tg rdomain
.Op no
.Ic rtable
.Op Ar table-id
.Op Ar name
.Pp
rtable when used with a specified table-id can execute services, diagnostic
commands (ping, traceroute) and set routes under alternate routing tables.
The default
.Ox
kernel can accommodate 256 rtables.
They have a 1:1 relationship with routing domains, except that routing domain 0
can contain multiple routing tables.
A routing table that is made before its routing domain,
.Xr rdomain 4 ,
exists belongs to routing domain 0.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. create routing domain 3, and its routing table, by putting a
loopback interface in it.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# interface lo3
nsh(interface-lo3)# rdomain 3
nsh(interface-lo3)# inet 127.0.0.1/8
.Ed
.It
configure a physical interface
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# interface em0
nsh(interface-em0)# rdomain 3
nsh(interface-em0)# inet 10.255.0.10/24
.Ed
.It
Once the rdomain has been initialized (by creating a loopback inside the
 rdomain) the administrator can add routes to the rtable.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# rtable 3 customer label network3
nsh(config-rtable 3)# route 0.0.0.0/0 10.255.0.1
.Ed
.It
Most daemons can also run in that routing domain; pf, bgp, ldp, rad,
ntp, resolv, motd and crontab run only in routing table 0.
E.g. to set up ssh in rdomain 3:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config-rtable 3)# sshd edit
nsh(config-rtable 3)# sshd enable
.Ed
.It
The rtable command also lets a user whose login class has operate run
ping and traceroute in the respective route domain / VRF.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> rtable 3
nsh(rtable 3)> ping 10.255.0.10
%PING 10.255.0.10 (10.255.0.10): 56 data bytes
