.Tg reboot
.Tg restart
.Ic reboot
.Pp
Restart the system.
.Nm
when processing the command will warn the user if there are
unsaved changes to the running-config.
In an interactive session, the user is asked to confirm the reboot.
Otherwise,
.Nm
refuses to reboot while there are unsaved changes.
The command needs the operate permission (see
.Sx Prompts, login classes and permissions ) .
.Bl -dash
.It
E.g. restart the system
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# reboot
% WARNING: The running configuration contains unsaved changes!
% The 'show diff-config' command will display unsaved changes.
% The 'commit' command will save changes to /var/db/nsh/nshrc.
Proceed with reboot? [yes/no]
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg halt
.Ic halt
Shut down the system.
The system will halt and then wait for a key to be pressed on the
console before rebooting.
As with
.Ic reboot ,
the user is asked to confirm.
It needs the operate permission.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. shutdown the system
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> halt
Proceed with shutdown? [yes/no] yes
% Shutdown initiated
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg powerdown
.Ic powerdown
Shut down the system and then turn off power.
Restarting the system will require access to the power-on button.
As with
.Ic reboot ,
the user is asked to confirm.
It needs the operate permission.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. power the system down
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> powerdown
Proceed with powerdown? [yes/no] yes
% Shutdown initiated
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg commit
.Ic commit
.Op Cm check | Cm confirmed Op Ar minutes
.Op Cm comment Ar text
.Pp
In
.Cm configure ,
.Cm configure private
or
.Cm configure exclusive ,
make the candidate the running configuration, then save the running
configuration to the permanent configuration space, which the next
startup of the system uses.
The differences between the running configuration and the candidate
are applied through the commands that read the saved configuration at
startup.
First the lines that the candidate removes are deleted, in the reverse
of the order the configuration is shown in.
Then the lines that it adds are applied, in that order.
A cloned interface the candidate has no block for is destroyed, and a
physical one has its lines removed.
If the running configuration then differs from the candidate, the
differences are shown as
.Cm show diff-config
shows them, and the commit is undone.
If another commit changed a top-level statement after the candidate
first changed it, the commit is refused once, with a message that names
each such statement.
Committing again keeps the candidate's changes in place of the other
commit's.
After a commit, the candidate starts again from the new running
configuration.
In
.Cm configure terminal
and outside configure,
.Cm commit
has
.Xr nshd 8
save the running configuration.
The last 50 saved configurations are stored for
.Cm rollback
and
.Cm show rollback .
Each is stored with who saved it, the
.Ar text
of
.Cm comment ,
if given, and its generation, which is one more than the generation of
the one saved before it.
A configuration that is the same as the last saved one is not stored
again.
.Cm check
and
.Cm confirmed
save nothing, and accept no
.Cm comment .
They need a candidate, so they are refused in
.Cm configure terminal
and outside configure.
.Pp
With
.Cm check ,
show what would keep the candidate from becoming the running
configuration, as far as it can be found in advance, and change
nothing:
.Bl -dash -compact
.It
an interface that a line names as a parent, carpdev, syncdev, patch,
trunk port, pppoe dev or bridge port, if the candidate does not have it
and it is not hardware that is present;
.It
a port in two trunks, a member of two bridges, or a bridge option,
such as stp, for an interface that is not a member;
.It
an address on two interfaces in one rdomain;
.It
a vlan's vnetid twice on one parent, or a carp vhid twice on one
carpdev;
.It
a daemon in an rtable whose table does not exist, if neither a route
in the table nor an interface in its rdomain creates it;
.It
daemon rules, new or changed, that the daemon's own test refuses, as
the daemon's edit command tests them.
.El
.Cm commit
checks the same first, and refuses a candidate with any of them.
.Pp
With
.Cm confirmed ,
the candidate becomes the running configuration but is not saved.
Unless
.Cm commit
is run again within
.Ar minutes ,
10 if not given and at most 1440, the running configuration is rolled
back to how it was before the first unconfirmed commit.
The rollback is logged with
.Xr syslog 3 .
.Xr nshd 8
performs the rollback, and demotes carp while it does.
A commit before the deadline saves the configuration and, once the save
succeeds, confirms it; so does
.Cm commit
outside configure.
If the save fails, the commit is not confirmed and the deadline still
applies.
If the save succeeds but
.Xr nshd 8
cannot read or remove the record of the unconfirmed commit, the commit
fails and says so, and the deadline still applies.
A system that is restarted before the deadline starts with the
configuration saved before the unconfirmed commit.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. save the configuration on the system
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> commit
% Saving configuration
.Ed
.It
e.g. commit, with a comment that says why
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config-private)# commit comment vlan 20 for the lab
% Saving configuration
.Ed
.It
e.g. check the candidate
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config-private)# commit check
% interface vlan10: parent trunk0 is no interface
% Configuration check fails: 1 problem
.Ed
.It
e.g. commit, and roll back unless it is confirmed within 5 minutes
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config-private)# commit confirmed 5
% Commit within 5 minutes to keep this, or it is rolled back
nsh(config-private)# commit
% Saving configuration
% Commit confirmed
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg rollback
.Ic rollback
.Op Ar N
.Pp
In
.Cm configure ,
.Cm configure private
or
.Cm configure exclusive ,
start the candidate over as the running configuration, which discards
its changes.
In
.Cm configure
and
.Cm configure exclusive ,
the candidate is shared, so every session's changes are discarded.
With
.Ar N
from 1 to 49, make the candidate the configuration saved
.Ar N
saves before the last, as
.Cm show rollback
numbers them, instead.
Changes that other sessions make after that do not appear in the
candidate.
.Cm configure terminal
has no candidate, and refuses
.Cm rollback .
.Cm show diff-config
displays what
.Cm commit
would change.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. roll back to the configuration saved before the last
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config-private)# rollback 1
% Candidate loaded from rollback 1
nsh(config-private)# commit
% Saving configuration
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg verbose
.Op no
.Ic verbose
.Pp
Verbose mode sets
.Nm
to display extra information on entered commands.
It is useful during a diagnostic and troubleshooting session.
.Bl -dash
.It
E.g. enable verbose output
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> verbose
% Diagnostic mode enabled
.Ed
.It
If you do not wish to have the extra information displayed, you may
disable verbose mode.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> no verbose
% Diagnostic mode disabled
.Ed
.El
.Tg show
.Ic show
.Op hostname | interface | autoconf | ip | inet | inet6 | route | route6\
 | sadb | arp | ndp | vlan | bridge | kernel | bgp | ospf | ospf6 | pf\
 | eigrp | rip | ldp | ike | ipsec | dvmrp | relay | dhcp | smtp | ldap\
 | monitor | log | system-stats | version | users | daemons | crontab\
 | scheduler | running-config | startup-config | active-config\
 | diff-config | candidate-config | rollback | environment |\&? | help
.Pp
The main diagnostic and informational command is 'show'.
show without arguments  displays the available diagnostic show sub commands.
.Bl -dash
.It
E.g using show and its built in help
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show
% Commands may be abbreviated.
% 'show' commands are:

  hostname          Router hostname
  interface         Interface config
  autoconf          IPv4/IPv6 autoconf state
  ip                IP address information
  inet              IPv4 address information
  inet6             IPv6 address information
  route             IPv4 route table or route lookup
  route6            IPv6 route table or route lookup
  sadb              Security Association Database
  arp               ARP table
  ndp               NDP table
  vlan              802.1Q/802.1ad VLANs
  bridge            Ethernet bridges
  kernel            Kernel statistics
  bgp               BGP information
  ospf              OSPF information
  ospf6             OSPF6 information
  pf                Packet Filter firewall information
  eigrp             EIGRP information
  rip               RIP information
  ldp               LDP information
  ike               IKE information
  ipsec             IPsec information
  dvmrp             DVMRP information
  relay             Relay server
  dhcp              DHCP server
  smtp              SMTP server
  ldap              LDAP server
  monitor           Monitor routing/arp table changes
  log               Follow daemon, messages, authlog or pflog, until ^C
  system-stats      System, firewall and kernel statistics
  version           Software information
  users             System users
  daemons           Daemons, and whether each runs
  crontab           Scheduled background jobs
  scheduler         Scheduled background jobs
  running-config    Operating configuration
  startup-config    Startup configuration
  active-config     Configuration of active context
  diff-config       Show differences between startup and running config, or running and candidate
  candidate-config  Candidate configuration
  rollback          Configurations saved, one of them, or what it changed
  environment       Show environment variables
  ?                 Options
nsh>
.Ed
.El
.Pp
Where
.Nm
has a session with
.Xr nshd 8 ,
nshd's system process runs a daemon's control program for a show, such
as
.Cm show bgp
or
.Cm show pf ,
and reads the DHCP leases.
Their output is not paged, and Control-C stops it.
Without a session,
.Nm
refuses these shows.
For
.Cm show system-stats ,
.Nm
runs
.Xr systat 1
itself, which needs the exec permission.
.Pp
.Ic show hostname
.Pp
Display the system's currently assigned hostname.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. display the name of the device
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show hostname
devicename.com
.Ed
.El
.Pp
.Tg interface
.Ic show interface
.Op Ar interface-name | Cm status
.Pp
Display essential information about the system network interfaces including
any network bridges / switches.
show interface without any arguments displays information about all
interfaces available on the system.
The
.Cm status
keyword shows a useful summary (including the Name, Admin Status, Link
state, and Media type) of each hardware and other
configured network interfaces on the system.
.Pp
show interface
.Ar interface-name
Display information about a specific interface.
.Bl -dash
.It
e.g. display information about the loopback interface.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show interface lo0
% lo0
  Interface is up (last change 22:07:02), protocol is up
  Interface type Loopback
  Internet address ::1/128, fe80::1%lo0/64, 127.0.0.1/8
  rdomain 0, MTU 32768 bytes
  4 packets input, 160 bytes, 0 errors, 0 drops
  4 packets output, 160 bytes, 0 errors, 0 unsupported
.Ed
.It
"Interface is up" means that the interface is turned on in software.
"Protocol is up" means that the interface is configured and ready to run.
The "Interface type" explains what the interface is used for on
the system.
Some interfaces are not intended to pass traffic for network users, and instead
handle internal functions on the system.
See
.Xr ifconfig 8
for more information on interface types supported by
.Ox .
.It
"Internet address" shows the IPv4 and IPv6 addresses configured for the
interface, if any.
IPv6 addresses with a % sign are "link-local" and not valid outside of the
context of the interface name specified.
MTU describes the Maximum Transmission Unit, the largest size of a packet which
the kernel can transmit on this interface.
.It
The statistics show the number of packets, bytes, errors, and dropped packets
in both incoming and outgoing directions.
The average input/output sizes are the mean size of the packets going in
and out of the interface.
The byte and packet counters are 64-bit.
An average smaller than 46 bytes, the smallest Ethernet payload, means
that the byte counter has probably rolled over, and it is not shown.
With verbose mode enabled, 'show interface' displays the raw kernel flags
for an interface.
See
.Xr ifconfig 8
for an explanation of these flags.
.Bd -literal -offset indent

nsh> show interface lo0
% lo0
  Interface is up (last change 22:13:28), protocol is up
  Interface type Loopback
  Internet address ::1/128, fe80::1%lo0/64, 127.0.0.1/8
  rdomain 0, MTU 32768 bytes
  4 packets input, 160 bytes, 0 errors, 0 drops
  4 packets output, 160 bytes, 0 errors, 0 unsupported
  Flags:
    <UP,LOOPBACK,RUNNING,MULTICAST>
  Hardware features:

.Ed
.It
With a bridge, verbose mode displays spanning tree member states and the
addresses the bridge has learned.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show int bridge0
% bridge0
  Bridge is up (last change 00:00:21), protocol is up
  Interface type Transparent bridge
  0 packets input, 0 bytes, 0 errors, 0 drops
  0 packets output, 0 bytes, 0 errors, 0 unsupported
  Flags:
    <UP,RUNNING>
  STP member state:
    sis0: listening

.Ed
.It
With an IEEE 802.11 wireless interface, 'show interface' displays the
network ID (SSID) and, if it is set, the power-saving period.
The network key is never displayed.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show int athn0
% athn0
  ...
  SSID mynet, powersaving (100 ms)
.Ed
.It
With an interface that supports media commands, including Ethernet and
IEEE 802.11 wireless interfaces, verbose mode displays which media types are
available.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show int sis0
% sis0
  Supported media types on sis0:
    media none
    media 10baseT
    media 10baseT, mediaopt full-duplex
    media 100baseTX
    media 100baseTX, mediaopt full-duplex
    media autoselect

.Ed
.El
e.g. briefly list the status of all interfaces on the system.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show interface status
% Name    Status  Link        Routing-Domain  Media
  lo0     up      -                        0
  em0     up      active                   0  Ethernet 1000baseT full-duplex
  enc0    down    active                   0
  pflog0  up      -                        0
  vether1 up      active                   0  Ethernet
  bridge101 up      -                        0
  veb201  up      -                        0
nsh>
.Ed
.Pp
.Tg autoconf
.Ic show autoconf
.Pp
Display the interfaces on the system that are dynamically configured and the
current state and configured settings such as IP addresses, routes
nameservers and the server that the system recieved the autoconf settings from.
.Pp
e.g. list current autoconfiguration state of the system.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show autoconf
em0 [Bound]
	inet 10.0.2.15 netmask 255.255.255.0
	default gateway 10.0.2.2
	nameservers 10.0.0.1 192.168.0.1
	lease 17 hours
	dhcp server 10.0.2.2
.Ed
.Pp
.Tg ip
.Tg inet
.Tg inet6
.Cm show
.Op ip | inet | inet6
.Pp
Display the IP addresses on all interfaces, their respective rdomains and
where the ip address was configured from on the system including:
.Bl -dash
.It
static
.It
dhcp
.It
autoconf, an IPv4 address that PPPoE's IPCP assigned, or an IPv6
stateless autoconfiguration address
.It
temporary
.It
link-local
.El
.Pp
Only a session with
.Xr nshd 8 ,
which can ask nshd's system process, sees what configured an IPv4
address; for anyone else, the address's type is empty.
.Pp
E.g show all ipv4 and IPv6 adddresses on the system
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh> show ip
Address      Interface  RDomain  Type
10.0.2.15    em0              0  dhcp
10.10.10.10  em1            200  static
127.0.0.1    lo0              0  static
127.0.0.1    lo0            200  static
::1          lo0              0  static
::1          lo200          200  static
fe80::1      lo0              0  link-local
nsh>
.Ed
E.g. show only IPv4 addresses on the system.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsh(config)# show inet
Address      Interface  RDomain  Type
10.0.2.15    em0              0  dhcp
